<article class="env-remark" id="08I4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08I4">Remark <span data-tag="08I4">20.42.14</span>.</a> Suppose that </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ X' \ar[r]_ h \ar[d]_{f'} &  X \ar[d]^ f \\  S' \ar[r]^ g &  S }  \]
</div>
  <p> is a commutative diagram of ringed spaces. Let $K, L$ be objects of $D(\mathcal{O}_ X)$. We claim there exists a canonical base change map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  Lg^*Rf_*R\mathop{\mathcal{H}\! \mathit{om}}\nolimits (K, L) \longrightarrow R(f')_*R\mathop{\mathcal{H}\! \mathit{om}}\nolimits (Lh^*K, Lh^*L)  \]
</div>
  <p> in $D(\mathcal{O}_{S'})$. Namely, we take the map adjoint to the composition </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \begin{align*}  L(f')^*Lg^*Rf_*R\mathop{\mathcal{H}\! \mathit{om}}\nolimits (K, L) &  = Lh^*Lf^*Rf_*R\mathop{\mathcal{H}\! \mathit{om}}\nolimits (K, L) \\ &  \to Lh^*R\mathop{\mathcal{H}\! \mathit{om}}\nolimits (K, L) \\ &  \to R\mathop{\mathcal{H}\! \mathit{om}}\nolimits (Lh^*K, Lh^*L) \end{align*}
</div>
  <p> where the first arrow uses the adjunction mapping $Lf^*Rf_* \to \text{id}$ and the second arrow is the canonical map constructed in Remark <a href="/tag/08I3" data-tag="08I3">20.42.13</a>. </p>
</article>